o = {\n  steps: Array<{ items?: Array<{ type: string }> }>;\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: unknown): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const quoteScenario = scenario as Scenario;\n  const firstItemType = quoteScenario.steps[0]?.items?.[0]?.type;\n  const basePremium = firstItemType ? BASE_PREMIUMS[firstItemType] ?? 0 : 0;\n  return { results: [{ premium: basePremium + PROCESSING_FEE }] };\n};\n```\n\nComponent Count:\n- Constants: 6 (5, 110, 66, 0, \"sword\", \"amulet\") = 6\n- Bindings: 6 (PROCESSING_FEE, BASE_PREMIUMS, scenario, quoteScenario, firstItemType, basePremium) = 6\n- Invocations: 0 = 0\n- Conditionals: 1 ternary = 4 (`??` is not counted per APP's listed conditionals)\n- Loops: 0 = 0\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\n\n**Total Mass: 16**\n\n**Mass Change: 19 → 16 (Δ -3)**\n\n## Simple Design Rules Applied\n1. **Tests Pass**: All 3 existing tests still pass.\n2. **Reveals Intent**: `BASE_PREMIUMS` makes the item-to-base-premium mapping explicit; `firstItemType` and `Scenario` clarify what data is being processed.\n3. **No Duplication**: Removed the repeated `return { results: [{ premium: ... + PROCESSING_FEE }] }` in every branch; the premium is now built once from a looked-up base value.\n4. **Fewest Elements**: Replaced two item-specific constants and two `if` branches with one map and one lookup expression. Added a small `Scenario` type alias to avoid an inline cast type, which improves readability without over-engineering.\n\n## Refactoring Applied\n- Introduced `BASE_PREMIUMS` lookup map to remove the `if/else` item dispatch.\n- Extracted a single result construction, removing duplicated return expressions.\n- Added a `Scenario` type alias to name the expected input shape.\n- Renamed `typedScenario` → `quoteScenario` and `itemType` → `firstItemType` for clarity.\n\n## Tests\n```\n✓ src/claim-office.spec.ts  (35 tests | 32 skipped)\nTest Files  1 passed (1)\nTests  3 passed | 32 todo (35)\n```\n\nAll tests green."}],"details":{"mo