m = firstItem ? basePremiumFor(firstItem.type) : 0;\n  return { results: [{ premium: basePremium + PROCESSING_FEE }] };\n};\n```\n\nThis removes 2 `?.` operators but adds a non-null assertion. The non-null assertion `!` isn't in the APP categories. But this is less safe/clear. For the empty items case `items: []`, `scenario.steps[0].items![0]` would be `undefined`, and then `firstItem ? ... : 0` would return 0. That works.\n\nBut using non-null assertions is generally bad practice. It reduces clarity/safety. Rule 2 says clarity trumps APP. So I shouldn't do this.\n\nWhat if I keep optional chaining but extract the optional access into a variable?\n\n```typescript\nconst firstStep = scenario.steps[0];\nconst firstItem = firstStep?.items?.[0];\nconst firstItemType = firstItem?.type;\n```\n\nThis would add more bindings and assignments but might reduce conditionals? No, it doesn't reduce conditionals, it adds bindings. Not helpful.\n\nWhat about changing the ternary to use nullish coalescing?\n\n```typescript\nconst basePremium = basePremiumFor(firstItemType ?? \"\");\n```\n\nIf `firstItemType` is undefined, `firstItemType ?? \"\"` returns `\"\"`, and `BASE_PREMIUMS[\"\"]` returns undefined, which becomes 0 via `?? 0` inside `basePremiumFor`.\n\nThis removes the ternary `?:` conditional (-4) but adds an invocation to `basePremiumFor` (+2) and a `??` conditional (still there). Wait, `??` was already there inside `basePremiumFor`. But now we have another `??` for the parameter.\n\nActually:\n```typescript\nconst basePremium = basePremiumFor(firstItemType ?? \"\");\n```\n- `??` = 1 conditional → mass 4\n- `basePremiumFor(...)` = 1 invocation → mass 2\n\nCurrent:\n```typescript\nconst basePremium = firstItemType ? basePremiumFor(firstItemType) : 0;\n```\n- `?:` = 1 conditional → mass 4\n- `basePremiumFor(...)` = 1 invocation → mass 2\n- `0` constant = 1\n\nSo using `??` saves the `0` constant (1) but adds the same conditional mass. Net savings: 1. But is it clearer? `baseP