ests. The current tests only test rune blocks, not moonstone blocks. If we generalize now, we might pass future tests, but we should be careful not to break current tests. Let me think about whether generalization would break current tests.\n\nCurrent behavior: `isRuneBlock` checks if all 3 items are runes. For 3 moonstones, it would return false, so base amount = 3 * 27.5 = 82.5, premium = ceil(87.5) = 88. But there's no current test for 3 moonstones alone, so no test breaks. The only current moonstone test is 2 runes + 1 moonstone, which doesn't form a block of 3 same types, so behavior unchanged.\n\nBut generalizing to `isComponentBlock` would mean any 3 identical item types get block pricing. Is that correct per spec? The tests suggest blocks apply to \"alike components\" and the only components are runes and moonstones (both 27.5 base). A block of 3 swords would then be priced at 66 instead of 3*110=330. But there's no test for that. Is that the intended behavior? Probably not - blocks are specifically for \"components\" (rune/moonstone), not all item types.\n\nSo we shouldn't generalize to all item types. We could generalize to all item types with base premium 27.5, which would cover runes and moonstones. But that's a bit implicit/clever and might violate Rule 2 (clarity). Better to keep it explicit or wait for the test.\n\nSo I think the safest, most intent-revealing refactoring is to introduce the `Item` type alias.\n\nLet me also consider renaming `isRuneBlock` to something that better reflects its current purpose. \"isRuneBlock\" clearly says it checks if items form a block of runes. That's accurate. Keep it.\n\nWhat about `processScenario`? It currently only processes the first quote step. The name \"processScenario\" is broad. Maybe `quoteScenario`? But there are claim processing todos, so `processScenario` will likely handle both quotes and claims. Keep it.\n\nWhat about `quotePremium`? It calculates the premium for a quote. Good name.\n\nWhat about `basePremiumFor`? Returns base premium for an item type. Good.\n\nOK so the refactoring will be:\n1. Introduce `type Item = { type: string };`\n2. Use `Item[]` instead of `Array<{ type: string }>` everywhere.\n\nLet me also consider: we could extract a `BlockSize = 3` constant which already exists as `RUNE_BLOCK_SIZE`. Good.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at the code, the type annotation in `isRuneBlock` is `Array<{ type: string }>` and in `quotePremium` it's `Array<{ type: string }> | undefined`. The `Scenario` type also has `Array<{ type: string }>`. Introducing `Item` removes duplication and reveals intent.\n\nLet me implement this and calculate mass.\n\nBefore mass: 101 (my calculation)\n\nAfter:\n```typescript\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 110,\n  amulet: 66,\n  staff: 88,\n  potion: 44,\n  rune: 27.5,\n  moonstone: 27.5,\n};\n\nconst RUNE_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst RUNE_BLOCK_PRICE = 66;\n\ntype Item = { type: string };\n\ntype Scenario = {\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n  steps: Array<{ op: string; items?: Item[] }>;\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (itemType: string): number => BASE_PREMIUMS[itemType] ?? 0;\n\nconst isRuneBlock = (items: Item[]): boolean =>\n  items.length === RUNE_BLOCK_SIZE && items.every((item) => item.type === \"rune\");\n\nconst quotePremium = (quotedItems: Item[] | undefined): number => {\n  const items = quotedItems ?? [];\n  const baseAmount = isRuneBlock(items)\n    ? RUNE_BLOCK_PRICE\n    : items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + basePremiumFor(item.type), 0);\n  return Math.ceil(baseAmount + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const quoteStep = scenario.steps[0];\n  return { results: [{ premium: quotePremium(quoteStep.items) }] };\n};\n```\n\nBindings after:\nPROCESSING_FEE, BASE_PREMIUMS, RUNE_BLOCK_SIZE, RUNE_BLOCK_PRICE, Item, Scenario, customer, yearsWithMHPCO, steps, op, items, basePremiumFor, itemType, isRuneBlock, items (param), quotePremium, quotedItems, items (local), baseAmount, sum, item, processScenario, scenario, results, premium, quoteStep\n\nThat's 25 bindings (was 28). Reduction of 3.\n\nConstants: 13 (same)\n\nInvocations: 17 (same)\n\nConditionals: 4 (same)\n\nLoops: 2 (same)\n\nAssignments: 0\n\nMass after = 13 + 25 + 34 + 16 + 10 = 98\n\nMass reduced from 101 to 98. Small improvement, but the main benefit is clarity.\n\nWait, I should double-check if `Item[]` vs `Array<Item>` matters. I'll use `Item[]` for fewer elements. `Array<Item>` would add invocation (Array) and binding (Item). `Item[]` just has Item binding. So `Item[]` is better.\n\nActually, in the return type `{ results: Array<{ premium: number }> }`, we could also introduce a `QuoteResult` type. But that's only used once, so it wouldn't reduce duplication. Leave it.\n\nLet me implement the change.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"**Refactoring Evaluation**\n\n**Naming Evaluation**:\n- `PROCESSING_FEE`, `BASE_PREMIUMS`, `RUNE_BLOCK_SIZE`, `RUNE_BLOCK_PRICE` — clear constants\n- `basePremiumFor` — clearly returns base premium for an item type\n- `isRuneBlock` — accurately checks for a block of 3 runes\n- `quotePremium` — clearly calculates premium for a quote step\n- `processScenario` — appropriate for a scenario processor that will handle quotes and eventually claims\n- Anonymous `{ type: string }` arrays repeated 3 times — reveals intent poorly; an `Item` type alias would clarify the domain model\n\n**Current APP Mass** (src/claim-office.ts):\n- Constants: 13\n- Bindings: 28\n- Invocations: 17\n- Conditionals: 4\n- Loops: 2\n- Assignments: 0\n\nTotal Mass = 13 + 28 + (17×2) + (4×4) + (2×5) = **101**\n\n**Refactoring Decision**:\n- Rule 2 (Reveals Intent): Introduce a domain `Item` type and replace the repeated `Array<{ type: string }>` annotations.\n- Rule 3 (No Duplication): The item shape is duplicated across `Scenario`, `isRuneBlock`,