Green phase."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:95","name":"read","arguments":{"path":".pi/skills/green/SKILL.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":633,"output":265,"cacheRead":62656,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":4,"totalTokens":63554,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784920159887,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-d4cad67b-db9d-4d23-a2e4-46f9b13ced66","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:95","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: green\ndescription: TDD Green Phase - Implement minimal code to make the failing test pass\n---\n\n# TDD Green Phase\n\nYou are now in the **Green Phase** of TDD. Follow these instructions to make the failing test pass with MINIMAL code.\n\n## Why minimality matters\n\nThe Green Phase deliberately writes the smallest implementation that turns the active test green -- even hardcoded returns, even obviously-incomplete logic. This is not laziness or naivete:\n\n- It **exposes refactoring opportunities** in the next phase. When the implementation does exactly one thing, structural smells are visible. Over-implemented green code looks \"already clean\" and hides design problems.\n- It **prevents premature generalization**. The shape of the right abstraction emerges from the second and third test, not the first. Implementing for hypothetical future tests locks in a design before its constraints are known.\n- It **keeps the red-green-refactor cycle short**. Each cycle pays a cost in tokens and context; over-implementing in green collapses several future cycles into one large one and loses the per-test feedback loop.\n\n## Your Mission\n\n1. Implement the **minimal code** necessary to make the failing test pass\n2. Use the **simplest possible solution** (hardcoded values are acceptable)\n3. Avoid adding features for future tests\n4. Verify all tests pass\n5. NO optimization, NO refactoring yet\n\n## Context: $ARGUMENTS\n\n## Green Phase Rules\n\n- **Minimal code only**: Just enough to pass the current test\n- **Baby steps**: Make the smallest possible change\n- **No future features**: Don't implement what future tests might need\n- **Simple is better**: Hardcoded returns are perfectly fine\n- **Tests must pass**: Verify all tests are green\n- **No refactoring yet**: Save improvements for Refactor phase\n\n## Process\n\n### Step 1: Analyze the Failing Test\n\nUnderstand what the test expects:\n- What input does the test provide?\n- What output does it expect?\n- What is the **simplest** way to produce that output?\n\n### Step 2: Write Minimal Implementation\n\nImplement **only what's needed** to make the current test pass:\n\n```typescript\n// For first test \"should return 0 for empty input\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  return 0; // Minimal - just make the test pass\n};\n\n// For second test \"should return number for single input\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  if (input === \"\") return 0;\n  return parseInt(input); // Still simple\n};\n\n// For third test \"should add two numbers\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  if (input === \"\") return 0;\n  const numbers = input.split(\",\");\n  if (numbers.length === 1) return parseInt(numbers[0]);\n  return parseInt(numbers[0]) + parseInt(numbers[1]); // Only now add logic\n};\n```\n\n### Step 3: Run Tests\n\nRun `pnpm test` and verify:\n- Current test now passes\n- All previous tests still pass\n\n### Step 4: Verify No Over-Implementation\n\nCheck yourself:\n- Did I implement features for future tests? -> Remove them\n- Did I add logic not demanded by current test? -> Remove it\n- Did I optimize prematurely? -> Simplify\n- Did I refactor existing code? -> Revert, save for Refactor phase\n\n### Step 5: Report Completion\n\n```\nGreen Phase Complete:\n**Implementation**: [brief description of what was added]\n**Result**: All tests now pass ([X] passing)\n**Approach**: [explain why this is minimal]\n\nProceeding to Refactor phase.\n```\n\n## Minimal Implementation Strategies\n\n### Hardcoded Returns (Preferred for Early Tests)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\nreturn 0; // Perfect - minimal\n```\n\n### Simple Conditionals (When Multiple Tests)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should return number for single input\"\nif (input === \"\") return 0;\nreturn parseInt(input); // Still simple\n```\n\n### Generalization (Only When Forced)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should add multiple numbers\" - NOW generalize\nreturn input.split(\",\").reduce((sum, n) => sum + parseInt(n), 0);\n```\n\n## Important Guidelines\n\n### DO\n- Write minimal code to make test pass\n- Use hardcoded values when appropriate\n- Take baby steps\n- Verify all tests pass\n\n### DON'T\n- Implement beyond what tests demand\n- Add features for future tests\n- Optimize prematurely\n- Refactor during Green phase\n\n## Psychological Resistance\n\nYou will feel resistance:\n- **\"This is too simple\"** -> That's correct! Minimal is the way\n- **\"Hardcoded values feel wrong\"** -> They're exactly right for early tests\n- **\"I should implement ahead\"** -> Resist this strongly\n- **\"This is inefficient\"** -> Actually accelerates development\n\nTrust the process. Simple steps compound into elegant solutions.\n\n## Completion\n\nAfter completing Green phase, proceed to Refactor phase:\n\n```\nGreen Phase Complete. Proceeding to Refactor phase.\n```\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784920163026},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Green: implement block discount for any component type. For this test, 3 runes + 3 moonstones. General rule: groups of exactly 3 alike components get block price 60. So for each component type, count items. For every group of 3, charge 60; remaining charge per item.\n\nBut minimal for this test? The test has 3 runes and 3 moonstones. I could hardcode this exact case. But the next tests (4 runes, 7 runes) are already passing without block. The test \"2 runes + 1 moonstone\" is passing. So implementing general block logic now might be OK, but green says minimal.\n\nActually, the simplest thing that passes this test and maintains others is to generalize the block logic: group items by type, for each type charge floor(count / 3) * 60 + (count % 3) * 27.5, sum.\n\nWait, but the block price in BASE_PREMIUMS is currently per-item values with first insurance. The block price 60 already includes first insurance? No, the block base is 60. With first insurance 10% = 6. So block price with first insurance = 66. But RUNE_BLOCK_PRICE = 66 currently. Hmm, the refactor agent set RUNE_BLOCK_PRICE = 66 which includes first insurance. That's inconsistent naming.\n\nActually wait, in the BASE_PREMIUMS, the per-component values include first insurance (27.5 = 25 * 1.10). But RUNE_BLOCK_PRICE = 66 = 60 * 1.10. So all values include first insurance. Then we add fee and ceil.\n\nFor general block logic, I need to compute:\n- For each component type, let count = number of items.\n- Full blocks of 3: floor(count / 3), but only if count === 3? No, the spec says \"A building block of 3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\" and example \"7 runes → 175 G base premium\" = 7 * 25 = 175, no block. So block applies only to exactly 3, not to multiple blocks of 3. Wait, but \"3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 G base premium (two separate blocks)\" implies each type has exactly 3, so each block applies. But 6 runes would be? The spec doesn't say. It only says \"block requires exactly 3\" in the 4 runes example. And 7 runes has no block. So it seems a block applies to a group of exactly 3 alike components, but if there are 6 of the same type, does it get two blocks? The spec doesn't clarify. I'll interpret \"block requires exactly 3\" as: a component type gets the block discount only if its count is exactly 3. If count is 6, it doesn't get block (per \"no block — block requires exactly 3\"). So general rule: if count === 3, charge 60 base; else charge count * 25 base.\n\nWith first insurance baked in: if count === 3, value = 66; else count * 27.5.\n\nFor the current test: runes count 3 → 66, moonstones count 3 → 66, total 132, +5 fee = 137, ceil = 137. Good.\n\nFor 2 runes: count 2 → 2 * 27.5 = 55, +5 = 60. Good.\nFor 3 runes: count 3 → 66, +5 = 71. Good.\nFor 4 runes: count 4 → 4 * 27.5 = 110, +5 = 115. Good.\nFor 7 runes: count 7 → 7 * 27.5 = 192.5, +5 = 197.5, ceil = 198. Good.\n\nSo I'll generalize component block logic in green. This is a bit more than minimal but necessary to pass the test and maintain others. Actually, I could hardcode the specific case of 3 runes + 3 moonstones. But that would break when we add other component types. Since the spec clearly defines the rule, generalizing is appropriate.\n\nWait, the green skill says \"Minimal code only\" and \"No future features\". But to pass this test, I need to handle 3 runes + 3 moonstones. The minimal way is to detect \"3 runes + 3 moonstones\" specifically. But that's ugly. Generalizing block logic by type is a reasonable minimal step given the domain rule.\n\nLet me implement grouping by type and applying block if count === 3 for any type.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.edit:96","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const RUNE_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst RUNE_BLOCK_PRICE = 66;\n\ntype Item = { type: string };\n\ntype Scenario = {\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n  steps: Array<{ op: string; items?: Item[] }>;\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (itemType: string): number => BASE_PREMIUMS[itemType] ?? 0;\n\nconst isRuneBlock = (items: Item[]): boolean =>\n  items.length === RUNE_BLOCK_SIZE && items.every((item) => item.type === \"rune\");\n\nconst quotePremium = (quotedItems: Item[] | undefine