n- Maybe rename `COMPONENT_TYPES` to `COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES` to clarify.\n\nLet's design refactored code:\n\n```\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 110,\n  amulet: 66,\n  staff: 88,\n  potion: 44,\n  rune: 27.5,\n  moonstone: 27.5,\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE = 66;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\n\ntype Item = { type: string };\ntype Scenario = {\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n  steps: Array<{ op: string; items?: Item[] }>;\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (itemType: string): number => BASE_PREMIUMS[itemType] ?? 0;\n\nconst premiumForGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => {\n  if (COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES.includes(type) && count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE) {\n    return COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE;\n  }\n  return count * basePremiumFor(type);\n};\n\nconst countByType = (items: Item[]): Map<string, number> => {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    counts.set(item.type, (counts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  return counts;\n};\n\nconst basePremiumForItems = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const [type, count] of countByType(items)) {\n    total += premiumForGroup(type, count);\n  }\n  return total;\n};\n\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (quotedItems: Item[] | undefined): number => {\n  const items = quotedItems ?? [];\n  const baseAmount = basePremiumForItems(items);\n  return Math.ceil(baseAmount + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const quoteStep = scenario.steps[0];\n  return { results: [{ premium: calculateQuotePremium(quoteStep.items) }] };\n};\n```\n\nMass comparison: Added two functions `premiumForGroup`, `countByType`. Removed first loop in basePremiumForItems? It now uses countByType loop. Net functions +2, loops same. Names better. Maybe not lower mass. But clarity improved. APP 