spec.ts > MHPCO Claim Office > should add curse surcharge for a cursed sword -- final premium 165 G\nAssertionError: expected { results: [ { premium: 115 } ] } to deeply equal { results: [ { premium: 165 } ] }\n\n- Expected\n+ Received\n\n  Object {\n    \"results\": Array [\n      Object {\n-       \"premium\": 165,\n+       \"premium\": 115,\n      },\n    ],\n  }\n\n ❯ src/claim-office.spec.ts:104:20\n    102|       steps: [{ op: \"quote\", items: [{ type: \"sword\", cursed: true }] …\n    103|     });\n    104|     expect(result).toEqual({ results: [{ premium: 165 }] });\n       |                    ^\n    105|   });\n    106|   it.todo(\"should add high-enchantment surcharge at enchantment 5 -- b…\n\n⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯[1/1]⎯\n\n Test Files  1 failed (1)\n      Tests  1 failed | 12 passed | 22 todo (35)\n   Start at  19:10:56\n   Duration  160ms (transform 29ms, setup 0ms, collect 27ms, tests 8ms, environment 0ms, prepare 41ms)\n\n ELIFECYCLE  Test failed. See above for more details.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784920256525},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Red marker.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"## Red -- Test 13: should add curse surcharge for a cursed sword -- final premium 165 G\n\nRed Phase - Compilation Error Prediction:\n- Test: \"should add curse surcharge for a cursed sword -- final premium 165 G\"\n- Expected: No compilation error\n- Reason: `processScenario` and `calculateQuotePremium` exist\n\nRed Phase - Runtime Error Prediction:\n- Test: \"should add curse surcharge for a cursed sword -- final premium 165 G\"\n- Expected: Runtime assertion error\n- Expected value: `{ results: [{ premium: 165 }] }`\n- Actual value: `{ results: [{ premium: 115 }] }`\n\nRed Phase Complete:\n**Test Activated**: \"should add curse surcharge for a cursed sword -- final premium 165 G\"\n**Compilation Prediction**: No compilation error Correct\n**Runtime Prediction**: Expected { results: [{ premium: 165 }] }, received { results: [{ premium: 115 }] } Correct\n**Result**: Test fails as expected with assertion error\n\nProceeding to Green phase."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:104","name":"read","arguments":{"path":".pi/skills/green/SKILL.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":641,"output":242,"cacheRead":68672,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":4,"totalTokens":69555,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784920256527,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-47530c72-63ad-44a7-b80d-89e4f68b4a94","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:104","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: green\ndescription: TDD Green Phase - Implement minimal code to make the failing test pass\n---\n\n# TDD Green Phase\n\nYou are now in the **Green Phase** of TDD. Follow these instructions to make the failing test pass with MINIMAL code.\n\n## Why minimality matters\n\nThe Green Phase deliberately writes the smallest implementation that turns the active test green -- even hardcoded returns, even obviously-incomplete logic. This is not laziness or naivete:\n\n- It **exposes refactoring opportunities** in the next phase. When the implementation does exactly one thing, structural smells are visible. Over-implemented green code looks \"already clean\" and hides design problems.\n- It **prevents premature generalization**. The shape of the right abstraction emerges from the second and third test, not the first. Implementing for hypothetical future tests locks in a design before its constraints are known.\n- It **keeps the red-green-refactor cycle short**. Each cycle pays a cost in tokens and context; over-implementing in green collapses several future cycles into one large one and loses the per-test feedback loop.\n\n## Your Mission\n\n1. Implement the **minimal code** necessary to make the failing test pass\n2. Use the **simplest possible solution** (hardcoded values are acceptable)\n3. Avoid adding features for future tests\n4. Verify all tests pass\n5. NO optimization, NO refactoring yet\n\n## Context: $ARGUMENTS\n\n## Green Phase Rules\n\n- **Minimal code only**: Just enough to pass the current test\n- **Baby steps**: Make the smallest possible change\n- **No future features**: Don't implement what future tests might need\n- **Simple is better**: Hardcoded returns are perfectly fine\n- **Tests must pass**: Verify all tests are green\n- **No refactoring yet**: Save improvements for Refactor phase\n\n## Process\n\n### Step 1: Analyze the Failing Test\n\nUnderstand what the test expects:\n- What input does the test provide?\n- What output does it expect?\n- What is the **simplest** way to produce that output?\n\n### Step 2: Write Minimal Implementation\n\nImplement **only what's needed** to make the current test pass:\n\n```typescript\n// For first test \"should return 0 for empty input\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  return 0; // Minimal - just make the test pass\n};\n\n// For second test \"should return number for single input\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  if (input === \"\") return 0;\n  return parseInt(input); // Still simple\n};\n\n// For third test \"should add two numbers\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  if (input === \"\") return 0;\n  const numbers = input.split(\",\");\n  if (numbers.length === 1) return parseInt(numbers[0]);\n  return parseInt(numbers[0]) + parseInt(numbers[1]); // Only now add logic\n};\n```\n\n### Step 3: Run Tests\n\nRun `pnpm test` and verify:\n- Current test now passes\n- All previous tests still pass\n\n### Step 4: Verify No Over-Implementation\n\nCheck yourself:\n- Did I implement features for future tests? -> Remove them\n- Did I add logic not demanded by current test? -> Remove it\n- Did I optimize prematurely? -> Simplify\n- Did I refactor existing code? -> Revert, save for Refactor phase\n\n### Step 5: Report Completion\n\n```\nGreen Phase Complete:\n**Implementation**: [brief description of what was added]\n**Result**: All tests now pass ([X] passing)\n**Approach**: [explain why this is minimal]\n\nProceeding to Refactor phase.\n```\n\n## Minimal Implementation Strategies\n\n### Hardcoded Returns (Preferred for Early Tests)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\nreturn 0; // Perfect - minimal\n```\n\n### Simple Conditionals (When Multiple Tests)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should return number for single input\"\nif (input === \"\") return 0;\nreturn parseInt(input); // Still simple\n```\n\n### Generalization (Only When Forced)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should add multiple numbers\" - NOW generalize\nreturn input.split(\",\").reduce((sum, n) => sum + parseInt(n), 0);\n```\n\n## Important Guidelines\n\n### DO\n- Write minimal code to make test pass\n- Use hardcoded values when appropriate\n- Take baby steps\n- Verify all tests pass\n\n### DON'T\n- Implement beyond what tests demand\n- Add features for future tests\n- Optimize prematurely\n- Refactor during Green phase\n\n## Psychological Resistance\n\nYou will feel resistance:\n- **\"This is too simple\"** -> That's correct! Minimal is the way\n- **\"Hardcoded values feel wrong\"** -> They're exactly right for early tests\n- **\"I should implement ahead\"** -> Resist this strongly\n- **\"This is inefficient\"** -> Actually accelerates development\n\nTrust the process. Simple steps compound into elegant solutions.\n\n## Completion\n\nAfter completing Green phase, proceed to Refactor phase:\n\n```\nGreen Phase Complete. Proceeding to Refactor phase.\n```\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784920258449},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Green: need to handle cursed items. The data model for Item needs `cursed?: boolean`. In basePremiumForItems, for each item, add curse surcharge if cursed.\n\nBut the BASE_PREMIUMS values include first insurance. The curse surcharge is 50% of base premium (without first insurance). Hmm, this is getting messy. Let me think.\n\nActually, the clean approach is to separate base premium from modifiers. But the current code has base+first-insurance baked into BASE_PREMIUMS. For minimal green, I can add curse as 50% of the base premium value (which includes first insurance). But that's wrong conceptually.\n\nWait, the test expects 165. Current sword returns 115 (100 base + 10 first insurance + 5 fee). To get 165, I need to add 50. So curse surcharge = 50. That is 50% of base (100), not 50% of (100+10). So I need to know the base without first insurance.\n\nThis means I need to restructure. BASE_PREMIUMS should contain actual base premiums: sword 100, amulet 60, etc. Then apply first insurance as a policy-wide modifier. But that would change all previous tests.\n\nWait, previous tests were computed with first insurance baked in. If I change BASE_PREMIUMS to actual base and apply first insurance separately, I need to make sure all tests still pass.\n\nLet me restructure:\n- BASE_PREMIUMS: sword 100, amulet 60, staff 80, potion 40, rune 25, moonstone 25.\n- For components: block base price 60.\n- For first insurance: apply 10% to policy base.\n- For cursed: add 50% of item base.\n- Final: add fee and ceil.\n\nFor previous tests:\n- Empty: 0 + 5 = 5. OK.\n- Single sword: base 100 + first insurance 10 + fee 5 = 115. OK.\n- Single amulet: 60 + 6 + 5 = 71. OK.\n- Single staff: 80 + 8 + 5 = 93. OK.\n- Single potion: 40 + 4 + 5 = 49. OK.\n- Single rune: 25 + 2.5 + 5 = 32.5 → 33. OK.\n- 2 runes: 50 + 5 + 5 = 60. OK.\n- 3 runes block: 60 + 6 + 5 = 71. OK.\n- 4 runes: 100 + 10 + 5 = 115. OK.\n- 7 runes: 175 + 17.5 + 5 = 197.5 → 198. OK.\n- 2 runes + moonstone: 75 + 7.5 + 5 = 87.5 → 88. OK.\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones: 120 + 12 + 5 = 137. OK.\n\nSo if I change BASE_PREMIUMS to actual base values and apply first insurance separately, all previous tests still pass. Good.\n\nThen for cursed sword: 100 base + 50 curse + 10 first insurance + 5 = 165. OK.\n\nSo green needs to:\n1. Change BASE_PREMIUMS to actual base values.\n2. Change component block price to 60 (actual base).\n3. Add policy base calculation (sum of item bases including blocks).\n4. Add first insurance (10% of policy base).\n5. Add curse surcharge (50% of item base per cursed item).\n\nThis is more than minimal for one test but is the correct generalization. However, green says minimal. But implementing just the cursed sword without restructuring would be hacky and break the design. I think restructuring now is the right move.\n\nActually, I can do it more minimally: keep BASE_PREMIUMS as is (with first insurance) but subtract first insurance when computing curse? That's messy.\n\nBetter: restructure as described. This is a bigger change but sets up the proper model.\n\nWait, the green skill says \"Don't implement beyond what tests demand\". But the test demands curse surcharge. To compute curse correctly, I need base without first insurance. The current design doesn't support that. So I must restructure. It's not implementing future features; it's implementing the current feature correctly.\n\nLet me do the restructure.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.edit:105","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 110,\n  amulet: 66,\n  staff: 88,\n  potion: 44,\n  rune: 27.5,\n  moonstone: 27.5,\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE = 66;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\n\ntype Item = { type: string };\n\ntype Scenario = {\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n  steps: Array<{ op: string; items?: Item[] }>;\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (itemType: string): number => BASE_PREMIUMS[itemType] ?? 0;\n\nconst premiumForComponentGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => {\n  if (COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES.includes(type) && count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE) {\n    return COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE;\n  }\n  return count * basePremiumFor(type);\n};\n\nconst basePremiumForItems = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const groups = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    groups.set(item.type, (groups.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const [type, count] of groups) {\n    total += premiumForComponentGroup(type, count);\n  }\n  return total;\n};\n\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (quotedItems: Item[] | undefined = []): number =>\n  Math.ceil(basePremiumForItems(quotedItems) + PROCESSING_FEE);\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const quoteStep = scenario.steps[0];\n  return { results: [{ premium: calculateQuotePremium(quoteStep.items) }] };\n};","newText":"const PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE = 60;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst CURSE_RATE = 0.5;\n\ntype Item = { type: string; cursed?: boolean; enchantment?: number };\n\ntype Scenario = {\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n  steps: Array<{ op: string; items?: Item[] }>;\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (itemType: string): number => BASE_PREMIUMS[itemType] ?? 0;\n\nconst premiumForComponentGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => {\n  if (COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES.includes(type) && count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE) {\n    return COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE;\n  }\n  return count * basePremiumFor(type);\n};\n\nconst basePremiumForItems = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const groups = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    groups.set(item.type, (groups.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const [type, count] of groups) {\n    total += premiumForComponentGroup(type, count);\n  }\n  return total;\n};\n\nconst itemModifierAmount = (item: Item): number => {\n  const base = basePremiumFor(item.type);\n  let modifier = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) {\n    modifier += base * CURSE_RATE;\n  }\n  return modifier;\n};\n\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (quotedItems: Item[] | undefined = []): number => {\n  const items = quotedItems;\n  const policyBase = basePremiumForItems(items);\n  const itemModifiers = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + itemModifierAmount(item), 0);\n  const firstInsurance = policyBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\n  return Math.ceil(policyBase + itemModifiers + firstInsurance + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const quoteStep = scenario.steps[0];\n  return { results: [{ premium: calculateQuotePremium(quoteStep.items) }] };\n};"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":1146,"output":2051,"cacheRead":69504,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":943,"totalTokens":72701,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784920258450,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-f0e9d213-0a53-4f57-96e1-c546dfa8d678","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.edit:105","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 1 block(s) in src/claim-office.ts."}],"details":{"diff":"  1 const PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\n  2 const BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n- 3   sword: 110,\n- 4   amulet: 66,\n- 5   staff: 88,\n- 6   potion: 44,\n- 7   rune: 27.5,\n- 8   moonstone: 27.5,\n+ 3   sword: 100,\n+ 4   amulet: 60,\n+ 5   staff: 80,\n+ 6   potion: 40,\n+ 7   rune: 25,\n+ 8   moonstone: 25,\n  9 };\n 10 \n 11 const COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\n-12 const COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE = 66;\n+12 const CO