ubsequent quotes yet. So we can't change behavior without breaking intent of future tests.\n\nBut wait, the test todos include:\n- \"should apply first-insurance surcharge on the first quote -- +10% policy base\"\n- \"should apply follow-up contract discount on second quote -- -15% policy base\"\n\nThe code currently applies first-insurance to every quote. This is technically not correct per spec, but tests pass. However, a refactoring should not change behavior. But maybe this isn't a refactoring issue.\n\nLet me look at potential Rule 2 improvements:\n1. `itemModifierAmount`: The function adds surcharges for cursed and high-enchantment. A more revealing name could be `itemSurchargeAmount`. But \"modifier\" is still accurate.\n\n2. `policyBasePremium`: This name might be misleading because it's the base premium calculated from items. `itemsBasePremium` or `baseItemsPremium` would be clearer.\n\n3. `premiumForComponentGroup`: This handles the block discount for component types. `componentGroupPremium` or `componentBlockPremium` would be clearer.\n\n4. `calculateQuotePremium`: The prefix `calculate` is redundant. `quotePremium` is sufficient.\n\nI think I'll do:\n- Rename `calculateQuotePremium` to `quotePremium`\n- Rename `policyBasePremium` to `itemsBasePremium`\n- Rename `itemModifierAmount` to `itemSurcharge` or `itemSurchargeAmount`\n\nLet me think about what is the most impactful. Actually, let me reconsider. The prompt says \"Recent changes: Added high-enchantment surcharge for level >= 5\". So the function `itemModifierAmount` was just expanded. We should ask: \"Has the function's purpose become clearer/more specific through the latest test?\" The function now adds curse surcharge AND high-enchantment surcharge. So `itemModifierAmount` is still accurate but could be more specific. But since more modifiers might come, `itemModifierAmount` is reasonable.\n\nActually, I see a clear duplication issue: `basePremiumFor` is defined and used. But there's no real duplication.\n\nAnother option: The `policyBasePremium` uses `Map` and `for...of` loops. Could we simplify? Not really without hurting clarity.\n\nI think the cleanest refactor is renaming for clarity. Let me do:\n1. Rename `calculateQuotePremium` to `quotePremium` (remove redundant \"calculate\")\n2. Rename `policyBasePremium` to `itemsBasePremium` (more accurate)\n3. Rename `itemModifierAmount` to `itemSurcharges` or keep. Let me think.\n\nActually, \"itemModifierAmount\" returns a single number which is the sum of all item-specific surcharges. It could be `itemSurchargeTotal` or `itemSurcharges`. But `itemModifierAmount` is OK.\n\nWait, I just noticed: the current code has `const firstInsurance = policyBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;` and adds it unconditionally. The spec says it should apply on first quote only. But tests pass. However, this is not a refactoring concern - it's a behavior issue. Refactoring shouldn't change behavior.\n\nBut actually, looking more carefully at the test todos, the first-insurance surcharge is part of policy-wide modifiers. The code already applies it. This is a premature implementation detail but it's making tests pass. Since the tests that would fail (e.g., \"should apply follow-up contract discount on second quote\") are still todo, the code passes.\n\nBut the refactor agent must not break tests. So I won't change the first-insurance behavior.\n\nLet me calculate current APP mass.\n\nCurrent code:\n```typescript\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE = 60;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst CURSE_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE = 0.3;\n\ntype Item = { type: string; cursed?: boolean; enchantment?: number };\n\ntype Scenario = {\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n  steps: Array<{ op: string; items?: Item[] }>;\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (itemType: string): number => BASE_PREMIUMS[itemType] ?? 0;\n\nconst premiumForComponentGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => {\n  if (COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES.includes(type) && count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE) {\n    return COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE;\n  }\n  return count * basePremiumFor(type);\n};\n\nconst policyBasePremium = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const groups = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    groups.set(item.type, (groups.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const [type, count] of groups) {\n    total += premiumForComponentGroup(type, count);\n  }\n  return total;\n};\n\nconst itemModifierAmount = (item: Item): number => {\n  const base = basePremiumFor(item.type);\n  let modifier = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) {\n    modifier += base * CURSE_RATE;\n  }\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) {\n    modifier += base * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE;\n  }\n  return modifier;\n};\n\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (items: Item[] | undefined = []): number => {\n  const policyBase = policyBasePremium(items);\n  const itemModifiers = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + itemModifierAmount(item), 0);\n  const firstInsurance = policyBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\n  return Math.ceil(policyBase + itemModifiers + firstInsurance + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const quoteStep = scenario.steps[0];\n  return { results: [{ premium: calculateQuotePremium(quoteStep.items) }] };\n};\n```\n\nAPP mass calculation (per function or total? Usually total file):\n\nConstants:\n- 5 (PROCESSING_FEE value) = 1\n- 100, 60, 80, 40, 25, 25 (BASE_PREMIUMS values) = 6\n- 3 (COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE) = 1\n- 60 (COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE) = 1\n- \"rune\", \"moonstone\" = 2\n- 0.1 = 1\n- 0.5 = 1\n- 5 (HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) = 1\n- 