e doesn't fully match current behavior. But I shouldn't change behavior.\n\nWait - let me re-read the existing tests. All tests pass. The todo tests mention first-insurance surcharge. The current code always adds 10%. Since no test checks the \"first quote only\" behavior, the code is currently passing by always applying it. That's a partial implementation.\n\nI should NOT change behavior. So I need to be careful.\n\nLet me think about what refactorings I can do safely:\n- Extract a function for grouping items by type\n- Improve naming of helper functions\n- Consolidate constants\n- Maybe make the quotePremium calculation more readable\n\nActually, let me look at the grouping logic. `itemsBasePremium` groups items by type. This could be extracted to `countItemsByType` which would reveal intent better.\n\nAlso, `quotePremium` calculates several things. We could extract:\n- `policyBase` from itemsBase (already there)\n- `totalSurcharges` (item surcharges)\n- `firstInsuranceSurcharge` \n- `loyaltyDiscountAmount`\n\nWait, `firstInsurance` in quotePremium is a bit unclear. It's calculated as `itemsBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE`. Since it's always applied (for current tests), maybe naming it `baseSurcharge` or `policyBaseSurcharge` would be clearer. But the constant name is tied to the concept.\n\nActually, I think the best refactoring is:\n1. Extract `countItemsByType` to make `itemsBasePremium` clearer\n2. Improve naming of `FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE` to `FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE` or similar - but this might be unnecessary\n3. Maybe extract a helper for `sum` to reduce duplication\n\nLet me think about duplication. There's duplication in:\n- `itemsBasePremium` totals using a for loop\n- `quotePremium` uses `reduce` for surcharges\n\nBoth are summing things. Could extract a `sum` function. But that adds an abstraction. The for loop in itemsBasePremium is for Map iteration; reduce is for array. We could unify.\n\nLet me think about mass calculation. I'll do detailed APP mass.\n\n## Current Code Mass Calculation\n\nLet me count carefully. Current code:\n\n```typescript\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE = 60;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst LOYALTY_THRESHOLD = 2;\nconst LOYALTY_RATE = 0.2;\nconst CURSE_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE = 0.3;\n\ntype Item = { type: string; cursed?: boolean; enchantment?: number };\n\ntype Scenario = {\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n  steps: Array<{ op: string; items?: Item[] }>;\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (itemType: string): number => BASE_PREMIUMS[itemType] ?? 0;\n\nconst premiumForComponentGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => {\n  if (COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES.includes(type) && count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE) {\n    return COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE;\n  }\n  return count * basePremiumFor(type);\n};\n\nconst itemsBasePremium = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const groups = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    groups.set(item.type, (groups.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const [type, count] of groups) {\n    total += premiumForComponentGroup(type, count);\n  }\n  return total;\n};\n\nconst itemSurchargeAmount = (item: Item): number => {\n  const base = basePremiumFor(item.type);\n  const curseSurcharge = item.cursed ? base * CURSE_RATE : 0;\n  const enchantmentSurcharge = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD ? base * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE : 0;\n  return curseSurcharge + enchantmentSurcharge;\n};\n\nconst quotePremium = (items: Item[] | undefined, customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number }): number => {\n  const quotedItems = items ?? [];\n  const itemsBase = itemsBasePremium(quotedItems);\n  const itemSurcharges = quotedItems.reduce((sum, item) => sum + itemSurchargeAmount(item), 0);\n  const firstInsurance = itemsBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\n  const loyaltyDiscount = customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD ? itemsBase * LOYALTY_RATE : 0;\n  return Math.ceil(itemsBase + itemSurcharges + firstInsurance - loyaltyDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const quoteStep = scenario.steps[0];\n  return { results: [{ premium: quotePremium(quoteStep.items, scenario.customer) }] };\n};\n```\n\nConstants:\n- 5, 100, 60, 80, 40, 25, 25 = 8\n- 3, 60, \"rune\", \"moonstone\", 0.1, 2, 0.2, 0.5, 5, 0.3 = 10\nTotal constants: 18\n\nBindings:\n- type params: itemType, type, count, items, item (in itemSurchargeAmount), quotedItems, itemsBase, itemSurcharges, firstInsurance, loyaltyDiscount, scenario, quoteStep\n- type aliases: Item, Scenario\n- Map generic types: string, number\n- Record generic: string, number\n- Destructured in for-of: item, [type, count], sum, item (reduce)\nLet me count bindings (variable/parameter declarations):\n1. itemType\n2. type (premiumForComponentGroup)\n3. count\n4. items (itemsBasePremium)\n5. groups\n6. item (for-of)\n7. total\n8. type (destructured)\n9. count (destructured)\n10. item (itemSurchargeAmount)\n11. base\n12. curseSurcharge\n13. enchantmentSurcharge\n14. items (quotePremium param)\n15. customer (quotePremium param)\n16. quotedItems\n17. itemsBase\n18. itemSurcharges\n19. sum (reduce)\n20. item (reduce)\n21. firstInsurance\n22. loyaltyDiscount\n23. scenario (processScenario param)\n24. quoteStep\n\nType aliases Item and Scenario aren't runtime bindings. Generic type parameters like Record<string, number> - the string/number are type references, not runtime bindings.\n\nSo runtime bindings = 24.\n\nInvocations:\n- BASE_PREMIUMS[itemType]: 1 (property access with dynamic key counts as invocation? Actually property access is not an invocation. Hmm. APP say