r.\n\nActually, looking at the test todos more carefully: \"should apply first-insurance surcharge on the first quote -- +10% policy base\". The current code always applies it, not just on first quote. But the name `FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE` matches the test concept. Maybe it should be `FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE` to be clearer that it's an additional charge.\n\nIdea: Simplify `itemSurchargeAmount` by extracting helpers for curse and enchantment surcharges. But that might be over-engineering.\n\nLet me consider a bigger refactoring. The code has several concerns:\n1. Constants and base premium lookup\n2. Component block pricing\n3. Item grouping\n4. Item surcharges\n5. Quote premium calculation\n6. Scenario processing\n\nThe `quotePremium` function is doing multiple things. We could extract:\n- `policySurcharges` or `totalItemSurcharges`\n- `loyaltyDiscountFor`\n\nBut the current code is already fairly clean. Let me think of a minimal but meaningful refactoring.\n\nRefactoring plan:\n1. Extract `groupItemsByType` helper to make the grouping logic explicit and reusable.\n2. Rename `FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE` to `FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE` and local variable to `firstInsuranceSurcharge` for clarity.\n3. Rename `itemSurchargeAmount`? It's already clear.\n4. Maybe rename `itemsBasePremium` to `policyBasePremium`? Hmm, \"items base premium\" is clear enough.\n\nActually, the most impactful refactoring for Rule 2 is extracting the grouping logic with a good name. Let me also consider using a `sumBy` helper for the reduce.\n\nBut I want to keep changes minimal. Let me do:\n1. Extract `countItemsByType` function\n2. Rename `FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE` → `FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE` and `firstInsurance` → `firstInsuranceSurcharge`\n3. Maybe rename `itemsBasePremium` → `policyBasePremium`? The function calculates the base for all items in the policy. That might be clearer. But it's a bit of a stretch.\n\nLet me reconsider. The function `itemsBasePremium` returns the sum of base