also look at `premiumForComponentGroup`. The name is good. But the condition `count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE` only gives discount for exactly 3. What about 6? The current logic doesn't handle 6 as two blocks. There's a test for 7 runes: \"should charge per component for 7 runes -- final premium 198 G\". Let me verify: 7 runes at 25 each = 175. First insurance 10% = 17.5. Surcharges 0. Processing fee 5. Total = 197.5, ceil = 198. Yes.\n\nSo the block discount only applies to exactly 3. That's per current tests. Future tests might test 6. But we don't implement future tests.\n\nThe component block logic is okay.\n\nLet me now implement the refactoring.\n\nActually, wait. Before making changes, I should evaluate if renaming `FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE` is safe. The constant is not exported, so internal rename is safe. Good.\n\nLet me also think about `COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES`. It's used in `premiumForComponentGroup`. Could we name it `BLOCK_DISCOUNT_ELIGIBLE_TYPES`? That might be clearer. But `COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES` is also clear.\n\nLet me write the refactored code:\n\n```typescript\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE = 60;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst LOYALTY_THRESHOLD = 2;\nconst LOYALTY_RATE = 0.2;\nconst CURSE_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE = 0.3;\n\ntype Item = { type: string; cursed?: boolean; enchantment?: number };\n\ntype Scenario = {\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n  steps: Array<{ op: string; items?: Item[] }>;\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (itemType: string): number => BASE_PREMIUMS[itemType] ?? 0;\n\nconst countItemsByType = (items: Item[]): Map<string, number> => {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of