- final premiums 115 G and 100 G\"\n- Expected: Runtime assertion error\n- Expected value: `{ results: [{ premium: 115 }, { premium: 100 }] }`\n- Actual value: `{ results: [{ premium: 115 }] }`\n\nRed Phase Complete:\n**Test Activated**: \"should apply follow-up contract discount on second quote -- final premiums 115 G and 100 G\"\n**Compilation Prediction**: No compilation error Correct\n**Runtime Prediction**: Expected { results: [{ premium: 115 }, { premium: 100 }] }, received { results: [{ premium: 115 }] } Correct\n**Result**: Test fails as expected with assertion error\n\nProceeding to Green phase."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:132","name":"read","arguments":{"path":".pi/skills/green/SKILL.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":626,"output":264,"cacheRead":86016,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":4,"totalTokens":86906,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784920470832,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-1595a0ab-b21f-4937-bf3f-ccbe80459ca9","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:132","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: green\ndescription: TDD Green Phase - Implement minimal code to make the failing test pass\n---\n\n# TDD Green Phase\n\nYou are now in the **Green Phase** of TDD. Follow these instructions to make the failing test pass with MINIMAL code.\n\n## Why minimality matters\n\nThe Green Phase deliberately writes the smallest implementation that turns the active test green -- even hardcoded returns, even obviously-incomplete logic. This is not laziness or naivete:\n\n- It **exposes refactoring opportunities** in the next phase. When the implementation does exactly one thing, structural smells are visible. Over-implemented green code looks \"already clean\" and hides design problems.\n- It **prevents premature generalization**. The shape of the right abstraction emerges from the second and third test, not the first. Implementing for hypothetical future tests locks in a design before its constraints are known.\n- It **keeps the red-green-refactor cycle short**. Each cycle pays a cost in tokens and context; over-implementing in green collapses several future cycles into one large one and loses the per-test feedback loop.\n\n## Your Mission\n\n1. Implement the **minimal code** necessary to make the failing test pass\n2. Use the **simplest possible solution** (hardcoded values are acceptable)\n3. Avoid adding features for future tests\n4. Verify all tests pass\n5. NO optimization, NO refactoring yet\n\n## Context: $ARGUMENTS\n\n## Green Phase Rules\n\n- **Minimal code only**: Just enough to pass the current test\n- **Baby steps**: Make the smallest possible change\n- **No future features**: Don't implement what future tests might need\n- **Simple is better**: Hardcoded returns are perfectly fine\n- **Tests must pass**: Verify all tests are green\n- **No refactoring yet**: Save improvements for Refactor phase\n\n## Process\n\n### Step 1: Analyze the Failing Test\n\nUnderstand what the test expects:\n- What input does the test provide?\n- What output does it expect?\n- What is the **simplest** way to produce that output?\n\n### Step 2: Write Minimal Implementation\n\nImplement **only what's needed** to make the current test pass:\n\n```typescript\n// For first test \"should return 0 for empty input\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  return 0; // Minimal - just make the test pass\n};\n\n// For second test \"should return number for single input\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  if (input === \"\") return 0;\n  return parseInt(input); // Still simple\n};\n\n// For third test \"should add two numbers\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  if (input === \"\") return 0;\n  const numbers = input.split(\",\");\n  if (numbers.length === 1) return parseInt(numbers[0]);\n  return parseInt(numbers[0]) + parseInt(numbers[1]); // Only now add logic\n};\n```\n\n### Step 3: Run Tests\n\nRun `pnpm test` and verify:\n- Current test now passes\n- All previous tests still pass\n\n### Step 4: Verify No Over-Implementation\n\nCheck yourself:\n- Did I implement features for future tests? -> Remove them\n- Did I add logic not demanded by current test? -> Remove it\n- Did I optimize prematurely? -> Simplify\n- Did I refactor existing code? -> Revert, save for Refactor phase\n\n### Step 5: Report Completion\n\n```\nGreen Phase Complete:\n**Implementation**: [brief description of what was added]\n**Result**: All tests now pass ([X] passing)\n**Approach**: [explain why this is minimal]\n\nProceeding to Refactor phase.\n```\n\n## Minimal Implementation Strategies\n\n### Hardcoded Returns (Preferred for Early Tests)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\nreturn 0; // Perfect - minimal\n```\n\n### Simple Conditionals (When Multiple Tests)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should return number for single input\"\nif (input === \"\") return 0;\nreturn parseInt(input); // Still simple\n```\n\n### Generalization (Only When Forced)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should add multiple numbers\" - NOW generalize\nreturn input.split(\",\").reduce((sum, n) => sum + parseInt(n), 0);\n```\n\n## Important Guidelines\n\n### DO\n- Write minimal code to make test pass\n- Use hardcoded values when appropriate\n- Take baby steps\n- Verify all tests pass\n\n### DON'T\n- Implement beyond what tests demand\n- Add features for future tests\n- Optimize prematurely\n- Refactor during Green phase\n\n## Psychological Resistance\n\nYou will feel resistance:\n- **\"This is too simple\"** -> That's correct! Minimal is the way\n- **\"Hardcoded values feel wrong\"** -> They're exactly right for early tests\n- **\"I should implement ahead\"** -> Resist this strongly\n- **\"This is inefficient\"** -> Actually accelerates development\n\nTrust the process. Simple steps compound into elegant solutions.\n\n## Completion\n\nAfter completing Green phase, proceed to Refactor phase:\n\n```\nGreen Phase Complete. Proceeding to Refactor phase.\n```\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784920472980},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Green: implement multi-step processing. For each quote step, determine if it's the first quote or follow-up. Apply follow-up discount (-15% of policy base) for quotes after the first.\n\nFor claim steps, we haven't implemented yet, so we can skip or throw. Minimal: process only quote steps for now. But the function signature returns results for all steps. For non-quote steps, maybe return something? But there are no claim tests active yet. We can just process quote steps and ignore others or throw. Since later tests will add claims, I should handle them. But for minimal green, I'll process quote steps and for claim steps, maybe return { payout: 0, remainingCap: 0 }? No, that might be wrong. Better to throw or just process quotes. Actually, the current test only has quote steps. So minimal: process all quote steps, return premium for each. For non-quote, maybe skip or error. Since no tests demand it, I'll just handle quote steps.\n\nActually, the type says steps have `op: string`. I can check if op === \"quote\". For now, only process quotes.\n\nImplement:\n```typescript\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const results: Array<{ premium: number }> = [];\n  let quoteCount = 0;\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      results.push({ premium: quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, quoteCount) });\n      quoteCount++;\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n};\n```\n\nAnd quotePremium needs to know if it's follow-up:\n```typescript\nconst quotePremium = (items, customer, quoteIndex): number => {\n  ...\n  const followUpDiscount = quoteIndex > 0 ? itemsBase * FOLLOW_UP_RATE : 0;\n  return Math.ceil(itemsBase + itemSurcharges + firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};\n```\n\nFor this test, quoteIndex 0: no follow-up. QuoteIndex 1: follow-up discount = 100 * 0.15 = 15. Premium = 100 + 0 + 10 - 0 - 15 + 5 = 100. Good.\n\nLet me add FOLLOW_UP_RATE.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.edit:133","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst LOYALTY_THRESHOLD = 2;\nconst LOYALTY_RATE = 0.2;","newText":"const FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst LOYALTY_THRESHOLD = 2;\nconst LOYALTY_RATE = 0.2;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_RATE = 0.15;"},{"oldText":"const quotePremium = (items: Item[] | undefined, customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number }): number => {\n  const quotedItems = items ?? [];\n  const itemsBase = itemsBasePremium(quotedItems);\n  const itemSurcharges = quotedItems.reduce((sum, item) => sum + itemSurchargeAmount(item), 0);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = itemsBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  const loyaltyDiscount = customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD ? itemsBase * LOYALTY_RATE : 0;\n  return Math.ceil(itemsBase + itemSurcharges + firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const quoteStep = scenario.steps[0];\n  return { results: [{ premium: quotePremium(quoteStep.items, scenario.customer) }] };\n};","newText":"const quotePremium = (\n  items: Item[] | undefined,\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number },\n  quoteIndex: number,\n): number => {\n  const quotedItems = items ?? [];\n  const itemsBase = itemsBasePremium(quotedItems);\n  const itemSurcharges = quotedItems.reduce((sum, item) => sum + itemSurchargeAmount(item), 0);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = itemsBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  const loyaltyDiscount = customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD ? itemsBase * LOYALTY_RATE : 0;\n  const followUpDiscount = quoteIndex > 0 ? itemsBase * FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_RATE : 0;\n  return Math.ceil(\n    itemsBase + itemSurcharges + firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE,\n  );\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): { results: Array<{ premium: number }> } => {\n  const results: Array<{ premium: number }> = [];\n  let quoteIndex = 0;\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      results.push({ premium: quotePremium(step.items, scenario.customer, quoteIndex) });\n      quoteIndex++;\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n};"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":1153,"output":1167,"cacheRead":86848,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":485,"totalTokens":89168,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784920472983,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-19d24ce3-d373-4021-b228-5cfc315540d0","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.edit:133","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 2 block(s) in src/claim-office.ts."}],"details":{"diff":"    ...\n 13 const COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\n 14 const FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\n 15 const LOYALTY_THRESHOLD = 2;\n 16 const LOYALTY_RATE = 0.2;\n+17 const FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_RATE = 0.15;\n 17 const CURSE_RATE = 0.5;\n 18 const HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\n 19 const HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE = 0.3;\n 20 \n    ...\n 56   const enchantmentSurcharge = (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD ? base * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE : 0;\n 57   return curseSurcharge + enchantmentSurcharge;\n 58 };\n 59 \n-60 const quotePremium = (items: Item[] | undefined, customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number }): number => {\n+61 const quotePremium = (\n+6