o: +1 ?? conditional at call site\nNet change: -1 binding, 0 conditionals\n\nHmm, the `??` moves from inside quotePremium to call site. Total mass same for conditionals. But we remove one binding. This is a small improvement.\n\nActually wait, if we change signature to `items: Item[]`, then the parameter binding `items` still exists, but `quotedItems` is removed. So -1 binding. And the `items ?? []` conditional is removed from quotePremium (but added at call site if we use `step.items ?? []`). Net: -1 binding.\n\nBut does this improve clarity? Slightly. `quotePremium` always receives an array, which is simpler. The caller handles the default. This is reasonable.\n\nLet me think of combining refactorings:\n1. Change `quotePremium` signature to accept `Item[]` instead of `Item[] | undefined`, and handle default at call site.\n2. Rename `premiumForComponentGroup` to something clearer.\n3. Extract `surchargeWhen` helper (if clarity improvement is deemed worthwhile).\n\nI want to make at least one refactoring that clearly improves the code. Let me focus on the most impactful one.\n\nActually, I think the best refactoring is to **extract a helper for applying percentage-based modifiers**. This addresses conceptual duplication between:\n- `itemSurchargeAmount` (curse, enchantment)\n- `quotePremium` (first insurance surcharge, loyalty discount, follow-up discount)\n\nBoth use the pattern: `base * rate` applied conditionally or unconditionally.\n\nHelper:\n```typescript\nconst modifierAmount = (base: number, rate: number, applies: boolean = true): number => applies ? base * rate : 0;\n```\n\nThen:\n```typescript\nconst itemSurchargeAmount = (item: Item): number => {\n  const base = basePremiumFor(item.type);\n  return (\n    modifierAmount(base, CURSE_RATE, item.cursed ?? false) +\n    modifierAmount(base, HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE, (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD)\n  );\n};\n```\n\nAnd:\n```typescript\nconst quotePremium = (items: Item[], customer, quoteIndex): number => {\n  const itemsBase = itemsBasePremium(items);\n  const itemSurcharges = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + itemSurchargeAmount(item), 0);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = modifierAmount(itemsBase, FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE);\n  const loyaltyDiscount = modifierAmount(itemsBase, LOYALTY_RATE, customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD);\n  const followUpDiscount = modifierAmount(itemsBase, FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_RATE, quoteIndex > 0);\n  return Math.ceil(\n    itemsBase + itemSurcharges + firstInsuranceSurcharge - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE,\n  );\n};\n```\n\nMass impact:\nAdded:\n- modifierAmount helper: bindings 3 (base, rate, applies), conditional 1\n- itemSurchargeAmount: invocations +2 (modifierAmount calls), bindings -2 (removed curseSurcharge, enchantmentSurcharge), conditionals -2 (removed ternaries, but still have ??)\n  Wait, before itemSurchargeAmount had conditionals 2 ternaries + 1 ?? = 3. After: 2 ?? + 0 ternaries (moved to helper) = 2. So -1 conditional in itemSurchargeAmount.\n- quotePremium: invocations +3 (modifierAmount calls), conditionals -3 (removed 3 ternaries) = -3 conditionals. But we still have the comparison conditions. Wait, the comparisons are still there as arguments. So the `?:` ternaries are removed. The comparisons themselves are not conditionals by my counting. So -3 conditionals.\n\nNet conditional change: itemSurchargeAmount -1, quotePremium -3, helper +1 = -3 conditionals. Good.\n\nNet binding change: helper +3, itemSurchargeAmount -2, quotePremium 0 = +1 binding.\n\nNet invocation change: itemSurchargeAmount +2, quotePremium +3 = +5 invocations.\n\nMass change:\n- Constants: 0\n- Bindings: +1\n- Invocations: +5 -> +10\n- Conditionals: -3 -> -12\n- Loops: 0\n- Assignments: 0\nTotal change: 1 + 10 - 12 = -1\n\nMass reduction of 1. Not huge, but the conceptual duplication is reduced and the code is more uniform.\n\nBut wait, does this improve clarity? `modifierAmount` is a generic helper