ter for mass, but two helpers are clearer. Or maybe I should not extract helpers at all and instead focus on something else.\n\n**Alternative: Improve `itemsBasePremium` with functional style**\nBefore:\n```typescript\nconst itemsBasePremium = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const [type, count] of countItemsByType(items)) {\n    total += premiumForComponentGroup(type, count);\n  }\n  return total;\n};\n```\nAfter:\n```typescript\nconst itemsBasePremium = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  Array.from(countItemsByType(items)).reduce((sum, [type, count]) => sum + premiumForComponentGroup(type, count), 0);\n```\n\nMass change:\n- Remove binding `total` (-1)\n- Remove loop `for...of` (-1 -> -5)\n- Remove assignment `total += ...` (-1 -> -6)\n- Add invocation `Array.from` (+1 -> +2)\n- `reduce` was already counted as loop/invocation. In the after version, reduce is the only loop. Before, reduce was only in quotePremium. After, reduce is in itemsBasePremium too. So +1 loop (+5) and +1 invocation (+2).\nWait, I need to count carefully.\n\nBefore itemsBasePremium:\n- Bindings: items, total, type, count = 4\n- Invocations: countItemsByType(1), premiumForComponentGroup(1) = 2\n- Conditionals: 0\n- Loops: 1 (for...of)\n- Assignments: 1 (total +=)\n\nAfter itemsBasePremium:\n- Bindings: items, sum, type, count = 4\n- Invocations: countItemsByType(1), Array.from(1), reduce(1), premiumForComponentGroup(1) = 4\n- Conditionals: 0\n- Loops: 1 (reduce)\n- Assignments: 0\n\nChange:\n- Bindings: 0 (total removed, sum added)\n- Invocations: +2 (Array.from, reduce)\n- Loops: 0 (for replaced by reduce)\n- Assignments: -1 (-6)\n\nTotal mass change: +2*2 - 6 = -2\n\nMass reduction of 2. But does it improve clarity? `Array.from(countItemsByType(items)).reduce(...)` is less clear than a simple for loop. The for loop is very readable. I think Rule 2 says keep the for loop. So this is not a good refactoring.\n\n**Alternative: Use a forEach instead of for loop?**\nNo, forEach is