t = 2.\n\nOld `processScenario` had inline `policyInsuranceSum(items) * 2`:\n- Extra invocation: `policyInsuranceSum` was already counted... wait, it's also used in `policyInsuranceSum` function. But in `processScenario`, the invocation `policyInsuranceSum` is now replaced by `policyCapFor`. So we replace 1 invocation with 1 invocation + 1 binding (items in policyCapFor) + constant binding. Net +2 mass plus the `2` constant is now `POLICY_CAP_MULTIPLIER` binding + constant value.\n\nActually, in old `processScenario`:\n- Constants: `2` = 1\n- Invocations: `policyInsuranceSum` = 1\n\nIn new `processScenario`:\n- Constants: 0 (the 2 is now in constant declaration)\n- Invocations: `policyCapFor` = 1\n\nBut we added:\n- `POLICY_CAP_MULTIPLIER` binding + constant `2`: mass 2\n- `policyCapFor` function: binding `items` (1) + invocation `policyInsuranceSum` (2) = 3\n\nNet change: old had 1 constant + 1 invocation = 3 mass in processScenario. New processScenario has 1 invocation = 2 mass. Plus new helper 3 + constant declaration 2 = 5. Net +2.\n\nHmm, but clarity improves because the business rule \"cap is twice the insurance sum\" is now named.\n\nThis is a Rule 2 improvement at the cost of APP. That's acceptable.\n\nActually wait, I need to include `policyInsuranceSum` itself. In old code, `policyInsuranceSum` was invoked in `processScenario`. In new code, `policyInsuranceSum` is invoked in `policyCapFor`. So the invocation just moved. No net change there.\n\nBut the constant `2` was inline in `processScenario` (old), now it's a named constant `POLICY_CAP_MULTIPLIER` at top level. So we added a binding but kept the constant.\n\nMass added: 1 binding (`POLICY_CAP_MULTIPLIER`) + 1 binding (`items` in `policyCapFor`) + 1 invocation (`policyCapFor` is called in processScenario) = 3.\n\nBut the old code had the invocation `policyInsuranceSum` in `processScenario`, new has `policyCapFor`. Both 1 invocation. So invocation mass unchanged.\n\nNet mass added by this refactor: 2 bindings = 2.\n\nTotal delta now: -10 (from before) + 2 = -8.\n\nStill negative. Good.\n\nActually, I realize I should be careful not to over-refactor. The `policyCapFor` is a nice clarity improvement but maybe not essential. But since I've already done it and tests pass, I'll keep it.\n\nLet me think if there are any other improvements. Looking at `processScenario`:\n\n```typescript\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): { results: Array<{ premium: number } | { payout: number; remainingCap: number }> } => {\n  const results: Array<{ premium: number } | { payout: number; remainingCap: number }> = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  let quoteIndex = 0;\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const items = step.items ?? [];\n      policies.push({ items, cap: policyCapFor(items) });\n      results.push({ premium: quotePremium(items, scenario.customer, quoteIndex) });\n      quoteIndex++;\n    } else if (step.op === \"claim\") {\n      const policy = policies[step.policy];\n      const result = claimResult(policy, step.incident);\n      policy.cap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n};\n```\n\nThe return type is very long. We could extract a `ScenarioResult` type:\n```typescript\ntype ResultEntry = { premium: number } | { payout: number; remainingCap: number };\ntype ScenarioResult = { results: ResultEntry[] };\n```\n\nThis would improve readability. But adds abstractions. Is it worth it? The type is used in `results` array and return type. So two occurrences. Extracting would reduce duplication slightly. But it's a type, not runtime code.\n\nActually, I think extracting the result type is a good Rule 2 improvement because the long inline type hurts readability. But it adds elements (Rule 4). However, since it's used twice, it reduces duplication.\n\nLet me do it.\n\nActually wait, I don't want to over-refactor. Let me think about whether this is necessary. The current code is readable enough. The long type is a bit ugly but not terrible. I'll skip this to avoid over-engineering.\n\nLet me also consider extracting a helper for `step.op === \"quote\"` and `step.op === \"claim\"`. But that's the main loop logic. Extracting might not help.\n\nI think I'm done. Let me do a final test run and read the final file.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:13","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":277,"output":1203,"cacheRead":26048,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1183,"totalTokens":27528,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784920683055,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-cfd569a1-5d99-40f9-a54f-fde9a689627d","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:13","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"const conditionalRateAmount = (\n  applies: boolean,\n  base: number,\n  rate: number,\n): number => (applies ? base * rate : 0);\n\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE = 60;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst LOYALTY_THRESHOLD = 2;\nconst LOYALTY_RATE = 0.2;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_RATE = 0.15;\nconst CURSE_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE = 0.3;\n\nconst DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\nconst DAMAGE_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst DAMAGE_ENCHANTMENT_RATE = 0.5;\nconst POLICY_CAP_MULTIPLIER = 2;\n\ntype Item = { type: string; cursed?: boolean; enchantment?: number; material?: string };\n\ntype QuoteStep = { op: \"quote\"; items?: Item[] };\ntype Damage = { itemType: string; amount: number };\ntype ClaimStep = { op: \"claim\"; policy: number; incident: { cause: string; damages: Damage[] } };\ntype Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n\ntype Scenario = {\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n  steps: Step[];\n};\n\nconst INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 1000,\n  amulet: 600,\n  staff: 800,\n  potion: 400,\n  rune: 250,\n  moonstone: 250,\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (itemType: string): number => BASE_PREMIUMS[itemType] ?? 0;\nconst insuranceValueFor = (itemType: string): number => INSURANCE_VALUES[itemType] ?? 0;\n\nconst countItemsByType = (items: Item[]): Map<string, number> => {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const item of items) {\n    counts.set(item.type, (counts.get(item.type) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n  return counts;\n};\n\nconst premiumForComponentGroup = (type: string, count: number): number => {\n  if (COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES.includes(type) && count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE) {\n    return COMPONENT_BLOCK_PRICE;\n  }\n  return count * basePremiumFor(type);\n};\n\nconst itemsBasePremium = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  let total = 0;\n  for (const [type, count] of countItemsByType(items)) {\n    total += premiumForComponentGroup(type, count);\n  }\n  return total;\n};\n\nconst itemSurchargeAmount = (item: Item): number => {\n  const base = basePremiumFor(item.type);\n  const curseSurcharge = conditionalRateAmount(item.cursed ?? false, base, CURSE_RATE);\n  const enchantmentSurcharge = conditionalRateAmount(\n    (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD,\n    base,\n    HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_RATE,\n  );\n  return curseSurcharge + enchantmentSurcharge;\n};\n\nconst quotePremium = (\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number },\n  quoteIndex: number,\n): number => {\n  const itemsBase = itemsBasePremium(items);\n  const itemSurcharges = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + itemSurchargeAmount(item), 0);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = itemsBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;\n  const loyaltyDiscount = conditionalRateAmount(\n    customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD,\n    itemsBase,\n    LOYALTY_RATE,\n  );\n  const followUpDiscount = conditionalRateAmount(\n    quoteIndex > 0,\n    itemsBase,\n    FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_RATE,\n  );\n  const subtotal =\n    itemsBase +\n    itemSurcharges +\n    firstInsuranceSurcharge -\n    loyaltyDiscount -\n    followUpDiscount +\n    PROCESSING_FEE;\n  return Math.ceil(subtotal);\n};\n\ntype Policy = { items: Item[]; cap: number };\n\nconst policyInsuranceSum = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueFor(item.type), 0);\n\nconst policyCapFor = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  policyInsuranceSum(items) * POLICY_CAP_MULTIPLIER;\n\nconst damageReimbursementRate = (item: Item): number => {\n  if (item.material === \"dragon\") return 1;\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= DAMAGE_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) return DAMAGE_ENCHANTMENT_RATE;\n  return 1;\n};\n\nconst damagePayout = (damage: Damage, item: Item): number => {\n  const reimbursement = damage.amount * damageReimbursementRate(item);\n  return Math.max(0, reimbursement - DAMAGE_DEDUCTIBLE);\n};\n\nconst claimResult = (policy: Policy, incident: ClaimStep[\"incident\"]): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } => {\n  let payout = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (!item) {\n      throw new Error(`Item type ${damage.itemType} is not part of the policy`);\n    }\n    payout += damagePayout(damage, item);\n  }\n  const cappedPayout = Math.min(payout, policy.cap);\n  const roundedPayout = Math.floor(cappedPayout);\n  return { payout: roundedPayout, remainingCap: policy.cap - roundedPayout };\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (scenario: Scenario): { results: Array<{ premium: number } | { payout: number; remainingCap: number }> } => {\n  const results: Array<{ premium: number } | { payout: number; remainingCap: number }> = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  let quoteIndex = 0;\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const items = step.items ?? [];\n      policies.push({ items, cap: policyCapFor(items) });\n      results.push({ premium: quotePremium(items, scenario.customer, quoteIndex) });\n      quoteIndex++;\n    } else if (step.op === \"claim\") {\n      const policy = policies[step.policy];\n      const result = claimResult(policy, step.incident);\n      policy.cap = result.remainingCap;\n      results.push(result);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n};\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784920688470},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The file looks clean. Let me do a final test run and then write the report.\n\nWait, I should also consider type formatting. The return type of `processScenario` is very long and could be broken into multiple lines. But that's cosmetic. TypeScript formatter would handle it.\n\nActually, looking at the file, I notice `conditionalRateAmount` is now formatted with a ternary on one line:\n```typescript\nconst conditionalRateAmount = (\n  applies: boolean,\n  base: number,\n  rate: number,\n): number => (applies ? base * rate : 0);\n```\n\nThis is fine.\n\nLet me run final tests.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.bash:14","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pnpm test","timeout":60}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":1487,"output":156,"cacheRead":27520,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":132,"totalTokens":29163,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784920688471,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-065b0fda-2e61-4841-b1c3-328b7ef260de","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.bash:14","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n> tdd-experiment-run@ test