Let me rename to `calculateBasePremium` or `basePremiumForItems`.\n\nLet me look for duplication:\n\n1. `BASE_PREMIUMS` and `INSURANCE_VALUES` both map item types to numbers. There's duplication of keys. Maybe combine into one structure with `{ basePremium, insuranceValue }`. But this might not reduce mass significantly.\n\n2. The validation `if (!(item.type in BASE_PREMIUMS) || !(item.type in INSURANCE_VALUES))` checks both. Since keys are the same, checking one would suffice. But the intent is to validate both. Could use a single source of truth.\n\n3. `basePremiumFor` and `insuranceValueFor` are nearly identical in structure.\n\n4. In `processScenario`, the loop mutates `policy.cap` after `processClaim` returns. Could `processClaim` handle the cap mutation? But it would need to know the policy is mutable. Actually, the current code does:\n   ```typescript\n   const result = processClaim(policy, step.incident);\n   policy.cap = result.remainingCap;\n   results.push(result);\n   ```\n   `processClaim` already computes `remainingCap`. The mutation is external. Could return just the payout and have the caller compute remainingCap, but that duplicates the subtraction. Or `processClaim` could mutate policy.cap directly. But that would change the function's contract.\n\n5. `componentBlockTypes` logic: `if (COMPONENT_BLOCK_TYPES.includes(type) && count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE)`. Fine.\n\n6. The `reduce` in `itemSurchargeAmount` could be extracted or made clearer.\n\nActually, the biggest issue I see is conceptual duplication between `BASE_PREMIUMS` and `INSURANCE_VALUES`. We could create a single `ITEM_DEFINITIONS` record that holds both values, eliminating the duplicate keys and validation logic.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully.\n\nCurrent code:\n```typescript\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\nconst INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 1000,\n  amulet: 600,\n  staff: 800,\n  potion: 400,\n  rune: 250,\n  moonstone: 250,\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (itemType: string): number => BASE_PREMIUMS[itemType] ?? 0;\nconst insuranceValueFor = (itemType: string): number => INSURANCE_VALUES[itemType] ?? 0;\n\nconst validateItemTypes = (items: Item[]): void => {\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (!(item.type in BASE_PREMIUMS) || !(item.type in INSURANCE_VALUES)) {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${item.type}`);\n    }\n  }\n};\n```\n\nRefactored:\n```typescript\ntype ItemDefinition = { basePremium: number; insuranceValue: number };\n\nconst ITEM_DEFINITIONS: Record<string, ItemDefinition> = {\n  sword: { basePremium: 100, insuranceValue: 1000 },\n  amulet: { basePremium: 60, insuranceValue: 600 },\n  staff: { basePremium: 80, insuranceValue: 800 },\n  potion: { basePremium: 40, insuranceValue: 400 },\n  rune: { basePremium: 25, insuranceValue: 250 },\n  moonstone: { basePremium: 25, insuranceValue: 250 },\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (itemType: string): number => ITEM_DEFINITIONS[itemType]?.basePremium ?? 0;\nconst insuranceValueFor = (itemType: string): number => ITEM_DEFINITIONS[itemType]?.insuranceValue ?? 0;\n\nconst validateItemTypes = (items: Item[]): void => {\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (!ITEM_DEFINITIONS[item.type]) {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${item.type}`);\n    }\n  }\n};\n```\n\nThis reduces duplication (keys only listed once) and simplifies validation. But it increases the number of object literals. Mass might go up or down.\n\nAnother improvement: rename `itemsBasePremium` to `basePremiumForItems` for better readability.\n\nAnother possible refactoring: extract the item definitions validation from the loop. Already done.\n\nLet me also look at the `rateBasedAdjustment` function. The first argument is `applies: boolean`. This is used as:\n- `rateBasedAdjustment(true, itemsBase, FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE)` - always applies\n- `rateBasedAdjustment(customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD, itemsBase, LOYALTY_RATE)` - conditional\n- `rateBasedAdjustment(isFollowUp, itemsBase, FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_RATE)` - conditional\n\nFor the first one, the boolean is always true. We could have a separate `rateAdjustment(base, rate)` function for unconditional surcharges. But that adds a function. Alternatively, we could remove the `applies` parameter and just have callers conditionally add the value. But that would introduce duplication of the multiplication.\n\nActually, the function is fine. It's a single responsibility: compute adjustment when condition applies.\n\nLet me think about `quotePremium`:\n```typescript\nconst quotePremium = (\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number },\n  isFollowUp: boolean,\n): number => {\n  const itemsBase = itemsBasePremium(items);\n  const itemSurcharges = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + itemSurchargeAmount(item), 0);\n  const firstInsuranceSurcharge = rateBasedAdjustment(true, itemsBase, FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE);\n  const loyaltyDiscount = rateBasedAdjustment(\n    customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD,\n    itemsBase,\n    LOYALTY_RATE,\n  );\n  const followUpDiscount = rateBasedAdjustment(\n    isFollowUp,\n    itemsBase,\n    FOLLOW_UP_CONTRACT_RATE,\n  );\n  const subtotal =\n    itemsBase +\n    itemSurcharges +\n    firstInsuranceSurcharge -\n    loyaltyDiscount -\n    followUpDiscount +\n    PROCESSING_FEE;\n  return Math.ceil(subtotal);\n};\n```\n\n`rateBasedAdjustment(true, ...)` is a bit odd. We could just compute `itemsBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE` directly. But then we duplicate the multiplication logic. Alternatively, extract a function for unconditional rate application.\n\nActually, maybe rename `rateBasedAdjustment` to `applyRateWhen`? But `rateBasedAdjustment` is descriptive enough.\n\nAnother improvement: The `customer` parameter is typed inline. Could extract a `Customer` type. This would reduce duplication and improve r