ce(...): 1 in policyInsuranceSum\n- insuranceValueFor(item.type): 1\n- policyInsuranceSum(items): 1\n- damageReimbursementRate(item): 1\n- Math.max(0, ...): 1\n- validateDamages(incident.damages): 1\n- incident.damages.reduce(...): 1\n- policy.items.find(...): 1\n- damagePayout(damage, item): 1\n- Math.min(rawPayout, policy.cap): 1\n- Math.floor(cappedPayout): 1\n- validateItemTypes(items): 1\n- quotePremium(...): 1\n- policyCapFor(items): 1\n- processClaim(...): 1\nTotal = 28\n\nConditionals:\n- rateBasedAdjustment ternary: 1\n- premiumForComponentGroup if: 1\n- itemSurchargeAmount: 2 ternaries via rateBasedAdjustment\n- quotePremium: 2 ternaries via rateBasedAdjustment\n- damageReimbursementRate ternary: 1\n- processClaim if (!item): 1\n- processScenario if/else if: 2\nTotal = 10\n\nLoops:\n- countItemsByType for...of: 1\n- basePremiumForItems for...of: 1\n- quotePremium items.reduce: 1\n- policyInsuranceSum items.reduce: 1\n- processClaim incident.damages.reduce: 1\n- processScenario for...of: 1\nTotal = 6\n\nAssignments:\n- countItemsByType counts.set(...): 1 (mutation assignment in loop body, but it's one assignment statement that executes per iteration)\nHmm, this is tricky. In APP, each assignment statement counts once regardless of how many times it executes. So:\n- countItemsByType: 1 assignment (counts.set)\n- basePremiumForItems: 1 assignment (total += ...)\n- quotePremium: 1 assignment (itemSurcharges = ...)\n- processClaim: 2 assignments (rawPayout = ..., policy.cap = ...)\nTotal = 5\n\nWait, also `const results = []` and `const policies = []` are bindings, not assignments.\n\nActually, `let total = 0` is a binding with initialization. Is that an assignment? I'd say the initialization is part of the binding. But `total += ...` is an assignment.\n\nTotal mass = 28*1 + 51*1 + 28*2 + 10*4 + 6*5 + 5*6 = 28 + 51 + 56 + 40 + 30 + 30 = 235\n\nNow let me look for refactoring opportunities:\n\n**Rule 2: Reveals Intent**\n\n1. The constants are all at the top. That's fine.\n2. `rateBasedAdjustment` is a helper that conditionally applies a rate. The name is okay but could be `conditionalAdjustment` or `applyRateWhen`. However, it's descriptive enough.\n3. `basePremiumFor` and `insuranceValueFor` use `?? 0`. The `?? 0` fallback means unknown types return 0. But `validateItemTypes` ensures they're known. The `?? 0` adds a bit of defensive programming but isn't needed after validation. It doesn't hurt.\n\n**Rule 3: No Duplication**\n\n1. There's some duplication in the validation: `validateItemTypes` checks both `BASE_PREMIUMS` and `INSURANCE_VALUES`. If both always have the same keys, we could simplify. But the current check is explicit.\n2. The `processScenario` function mutates `policy.cap` after processClaim. That's a side effect. Could be cleaner but works.\n3. The base premiums and insurance values are two separate records with the same keys. Could combine them into one record with both values, reducing duplication of keys. This is a good refactoring candidate.\n\n**Rule 4: Fewest Elements**\n\n1. Could combine `BASE_PREMIUMS` and `INSURANCE_VALUES` into one structure. This would reduce elements and potential inconsistency.\n2. The `rateBasedAdjustment` helper is small and functional. Keep it.\n3. Could simplify `processClaim` slightly.\n\n**Refactoring candidate: Combine BASE_PREMIUMS and INSURANCE_VALUES**\n\nCurrently:\n```typescript\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = { ...6 entries... };\nconst INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number> = { ...6 entries... };\n```\n\nCould be:\n```typescript\ntype ItemDefinition = { basePremium: number; insuranceValue: number };\nconst ITEM_DEFINITIONS: Record<string, ItemDefinition> = {\n  sword: { basePremium: 100, insuranceValue: 1000 },\n  amulet: { basePremium: 60, insuranceValue: 600 },\n  staff: { basePremium: 80, insuranceValue: 800 },\n  potion: { basePremium: 40, insuranceValue: 400 },\n  rune: { basePremium: 25, insuranceValue: 250 },\n  moonstone: { basePremium: 25, insuranceValue: 250 },\n};\n```\n\nThis reduces duplication of keys. Then `basePremiumFor` and `insuranceValueFor` would access the same object.\n\n**Refactoring candidate: Rename `rateBasedAdjustment`?**\n\n`rateBasedAdjustment` returns `base * rate` if applies, else 0. It's used for:\n- curse surcharge\n- enchantment surcharge\n- loyalty discount\n- follow-up discount\n\nSo it's a conditional rate application. The name is reasonable.\n\n**Refactoring candidate: Simplify `quotePremium` calculation**\n\nThe `quotePremium` has many intermediate variables. Could extract a function for the first insurance surcharge, but it's already clear.\n\n**Refactoring candidate: Use `Math.max(0, ...)` consistently**\n\n`damagePayout` uses `Math.max(0, reimbursement - deductible)`. Good.\n\n**Refactoring candidate: Avoid mutating policy.cap**\n\nInstead of mutating the policy in processClaim, we could return the new policy state. But that would require restructuring processScenario. Might not be worth it.\n\n**Best refactoring: Combine the two item definition records**\n\nThis reduces duplication (Rule 3) and fewer elements (Rule 4). It also improves intent because it makes clear that base premium and insurance value are paired properties of an item type.\n\nLet me plan this refactoring carefully:\n\n1. Define a combined `ItemDefinition` type and `ITEM_DEFINITIONS` record.\n2. Update `basePremiumFor` to use `ITEM_DEFINITIONS[itemType]?.basePremium ?? 0`.\n3. Update `insuranceValueFor` to use `ITEM_DEFINITIONS[itemType]?.insuranceValue ?? 0`.\n4. Update `validateItemTypes` to check `item.type in ITEM_DEFINITIONS`.\n\nThis reduces constants (fewer object literal keys duplicated) and reduces duplication.\n\nLet me calculate the new mass:\n\nConstants before: 28\n- BASE_PREMIUMS: 6 literals\n- INSURANCE_VALUES: 6 literals\n- Total object literals: 12\nAfter combining:\n- ITEM_DEFINITIONS: 6 item type strings as keys, and for each: basePremium value, insuranceValue value = 6 + 6 + 6 = 18 literals\nWait, the keys are strings which are constants too. So before: 6 keys + 6 values + 6 keys + 6 values = 24 constants. After: 6 keys + 6 base values + 6 insurance values = 18 constants. We save 6 constants.\n\nBut we add the type definition `ItemDefinition` which doesn't count toward mass (it's a type).\n\nBindings: same (51)\nInvocations: same (28)\nConditionals: same (10)\nLoops: same (6)\nAssignments: same (5)\n\nNew mass = (28-6)*1 + 51 + 56 + 40 + 30 + 30 = 22 + 51 + 56 + 40 + 30 + 30 = 229\n\nMass change: 235 -> 229, delta -6.\n\nActually, I need to count constants more carefully. In `BASE_PREMIUMS: { sword: 100, ... }`, the values are numeric literals (6) and the keys are string literals (6). In `INSURANCE_VALUES: { sword: 1000, ... }`, same (12). After combining, we have 6 keys + 6 base premium values + 6 insurance values = 18. So we save 6 constants. \n\nBut wait, do object keys count as constants? In the APP description, constants are literal values like `5`, `\"hello\"`, `true`. Object property names are string literals, so I'd count them. Yes.\n\nLet me also consider: is there any duplication beyond the keys? The two records represent the same domain concept (item type definitions). Combining them is a clear improvement.\n\nLet me also consider other refactorings:\n\n**Refactoring candidate: Extract policy update in processScenario**\n\nCurrently:\n```typescript\nconst result = processClaim(policy, step.incident);\npolicy.cap = result.remainingCap;\nresults.push(result);\n```\n\nWe could have `processClaim` return a function that updates the policy, but that doesn't improve much. Or we could rename `processClaim` to `processClaimAndUpdatePolicy`, but mutating policy inside processClaim is clearer? Actually, the mutation is already inside processScenario, not processClaim. Wait, processClaim returns remainingCap but doesn't mutate policy.cap. The mutation happens in processScenario. That's actually fine.\n\n**Refactoring candidate: Simplify `processClaim`**\n\n```typescript\nconst processClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: ClaimStep[\"incident\"]): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } => {\n  validateDamages(incident.damages);\n  const rawPayout = incident.damages.reduce((sum, damage) => {\n    const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (!item) {\n      throw new Error(`Item type ${damage.itemType} is not part of the policy`);\n    }\n    return sum + damagePayout(damage, item);\n  }, 0);\n  const cappedPayout = Math.min(rawPayout, policy.cap);\n  const roundedPayout = Math.floor(cappedPayout);\n  return { payout: roundedPayout, remainingCap: policy.cap - roundedPayout };\n};\n```\n\nCould combine cappedPayout and roundedPayout:\n```typescript\nconst payout = Math.floor(Math.min(rawPayout, policy.cap));\nreturn { payout, remainingCap: policy.cap - payout };\n```\n\nThis reduces bindings by 1 and invocations by 1 (Math.floor and Math.min become one expression but still 2 invocations, just no intermediate cappedPayout binding). Actually:\n- Before: bindings cappedPayout, roundedPayout (2), invocations Math.min, Math.floor (2)\n- After: binding payout (1), invocations Math.min, Math.floor (2)\nSo saves 1 binding.\n\nMass change: -1.\n\nBut this might reduce clarity slightly because `cappedPayout` and `roundedPayout` explain the steps. However, the combined version is still readable. This is a small improvement.\n\n**Refactoring candidate: Extract claim validation**\n\nThe validation of item existence in policy is done inside the reduce. Could extract a `findPolicyItem` helper:\n```typescript\nconst findPolicyItem = (items: Item[], itemType: string): Item => {\n  const item = items.find((i) => i.type === itemType);\n  if (!item) throw new Error(`Item type ${itemType} is not part of the policy`);\n  return item;\n};\n```\n\nThis adds a function but removes duplication of error throwing logic. Currently it's only used once, so it doesn't reduce duplication. It might improve intent by naming the operation. But adding a function increases elements. Not worth it for single use.\n\n**Refactoring candidate: Combine damage reimbursement logic**\n\n`damageReimbursementRate` and `damagePayout` are separate. Could inline, but separate functions are clearer.\n\n**Refactoring candidate: Simplify quotePremium firstInsuranceSurcharge**\n\n`const firstInsuranceSurcharge = itemsBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE;`\nThis always applies (no condition). We could use `rateBasedAdjustment(true, itemsBase, FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE)` to be consistent, but that adds an invocation and conditional. The current direct multiplication is simpler and clearer. Keep as is.\n\n**Refactoring candidate: Type definitions**\n\nCould move type definitions together. Currently they're scattered. Minor.\n\n**Best refactoring decision:**\n\n1. Combine `BASE_PREMIUMS` and `INSURANCE_VALUES` into `ITEM_DEFINITIONS` (reduces duplication and elements, improves intent).\n2. Simplify `processClaim` by removing `cappedPayout` intermediate variable (reduces mass slightly, still clear).\n\nLet me also think about naming of `basePremiumFor` and `insuranceValueFor` after the change. They still make sense.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the naming evaluation more thoroughly. The main function `processScenario` processes a scenario of insurance quotes and claims. That's clear. But maybe `processInsuranceScenario` would be clearer? The test file is `claim-office.spec.ts` and the module is `claim-office.ts`. The function name `processScenario` is fine in context.\n\n`rateBasedAdjustment`: returns `base * rate` if applies, else 0. This is used for both surcharges and discounts. The name accurately describes it. Keep.\n\nLet me proceed with the refactorings. I need to:\n1. Make the change\n2. Run tests\n3. Calculate new mass\n\nLet me first apply the combined item definitions change.\n\nActually, I should also check if combining the records could affect validation. Currently `validateItemTypes` checks both records. After combining, checking `item.type in ITEM_DEFINITIONS` is equivalent if we always maintain both values together. That's the intent.\n\nLet me write the new implementation file.\n\nCurrent:\n```typescript\nconst BASE_PREMIUMS: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n  rune: 25,\n  moonstone: 25,\n};\n\nconst INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 1000,\n  amulet: 600,\n  staff: 800,\n  potion: 400,\n  rune: 250,\n  moonstone: 250,\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (itemType: string): number => BASE_PREMIUMS[itemType] ?? 0;\nconst insuranceValueFor = (itemType: string): number => INSURANCE_VALUES[itemType] ?? 0;\n\nconst validateItemTypes = (items: Item[]): void => {\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (!(item.type in BASE_PREMIUMS) || !(item.type in INSURANCE_VALUES)) {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${item.type}`);\n    }\n  }\n};\n```\n\nNew:\n```typescript\ntype ItemDefinition = { basePremium: number; insuranceValue: number };\n\nconst ITEM_DEFINITIONS: Record<string, ItemDefinition> = {\n  sword: { basePremium: 100, insuranceValue: 1000 },\n  amulet: { basePremium: 60, insuranceValue: 600 },\n  staff: { basePremium: 80, insuranceValue: 800 },\n  potion: { basePremium: 40, insuranceValue: 400 },\n  rune: { basePremium: 25, insuranceValue: 250 },\n  moonstone: { basePremium: 25, insuranceValue: 250 },\n};\n\nconst basePremiumFor = (itemType: string): number => ITEM_DEFINITIONS[itemType]?.basePremium ?? 0;\nconst insuranceValueFor = (itemType: string): number => ITEM_DEFINITIONS[itemType]?.insuranceValue ?? 0;\n\nconst validateItemTypes = (items: Item[]): void => {\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (!(item.type in ITEM_DEFINITIONS)) {\n      throw new Error(`Unknown item type: ${item.type}`);\n    }\n  }\n};\n```\n\nAnd simplify processClaim:\n```typescript\nconst processClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: ClaimStep[\"incident\"]): { payout: number; remainingCap: number } => {\n  validateDamages(incident.damages);\n  const rawPayout = incident.damages.reduce((sum, damage) => {\n    const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (!item) {\n      throw new Error(`Item type ${damage.itemType} is not part of the policy`);\n    }\n    return sum + damagePayout(damage, item);\n  }, 0);\n  const payout = Math.floor(Math.min(rawPayout, policy.cap));\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.cap - payout };\n};\n```\n\nMass change:\n- Constants: -6 (from combining records)\n- Bindings: -1 (removed cappedPayout)\n- Total: -7\n- New mass: 235 - 7 = 228\n\nWait, I need to recount invocations. The optional chaining `ITEM_DEFINITIONS[itemType]?.basePremium` introduces an invocation? No, optional chaining isn't a function call. It's a property access. So no change to invocations.\n\nActually, does `?.` count as a conditional? It's a conditional property access. In APP mass, conditionals are `if`, `switch`, `?:`. Optional chaining compiles to a conditional but in source form it's not counted as a conditional explicitly. I think we don't count `?.` as a conditional for APP mass. So no change.\n\nLet me also consider if combining records affects type safety. TypeScript will be happy.\n\nNow let me apply these changes.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"## Refactoring Evaluation\n\n**Naming Evaluation:**\n- Current main function: `processScenario` — clearly processes an insurance scenario of quotes and claims. Fits its purpose.\n- `rateBasedAdjustment` — conditionally applies `base * rate`; name accurately describes the conditional rate adjustment.\n- `basePremiumFor` / `insuranceValueFor` — clear accessors.\n- `processClaim` — clear.\n- Assessment: Names adequately reveal intent; no rename needed.\n\n**Current Code Mass Calculation:**\n- Constants: 28\n- Bindings: 51\n- Invocations: 28\n- Conditionals: 10\n- Loops: 6\n- Assignments: 5\n- **Total Mass: 235**\n\nI’ll apply two 